  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Reflection positivity>

    \;

    <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-R\>\<times\>N>, <math|N=\<bbb-R\><rsup|d-1>> or
    <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d-1><rsub|L>>; <math|\<Theta\>*<around|(|\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>=<around|(|-\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>>;
    <math|A=-\<partial\><rsub|\<tau\>><rsup|2>+B>,
    <math|B=m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|N>>, <math|\<omega\>=B<rsup|1/2>>.

    <\proposition*>
      (a) <math|C*<around|(|\<tau\>,\<tau\><rprime|'>|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\||\<tau\>-\<tau\><rprime|'>|\|>*\<omega\>><around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>>
      (operator-valued kernel).<next-line>(b) If
      <math|<math-up|supp>f\<subset\><around|{|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0|}>>:

      <\equation*>
        <around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>*f,C*f|\<rangle\>>=<around*|\||<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1/2><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*\<omega\>>f(\<tau\>,\<cdot\><around*|)|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<tau\>|\|><rsup|2><rsub|L<rsup|2><around|(|N|)>>\<geq\>0.|\<nobracket\>>
      </equation*>

      (c) <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> satisfies
      OS0\UOS4.
    </proposition*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Proof.> (a) <math|<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>k*t><around|(|k<rsup|2>+w<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>*<frac|\<mathd\>k|2*\<pi\>>=<frac|\<mathe\><rsup|-w<around|\||t|\|>>|2*w>>
    and spectral calculus. (b) For <math|\<tau\>\<less\>0\<less\>\<tau\><rprime|'>>,
    <math|<around|\||\<tau\>-\<tau\><rprime|'>|\|>=<around|\||\<tau\>|\|>+\<tau\><rprime|'>>:
    the kernel factorises. (c) OS3: <math|<wide|\<mu\>|^>*<around|(|f<rsub|i>-\<Theta\>*f<rsub|j>|)>=a<rsub|i>*a<rsub|j>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>*f<rsub|i>,C*f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>>>;
    Schur.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The free field, reconstructed>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item>OS reconstruction of <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> gives the <strong|free
      field of mass <math|m>>: Fock space over
      <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|N|)>>, <math|H=\<mathd\>\<Gamma\><around|(|\<omega\>|)>>
      (Nelson's free Markov field).

      <item>The <strong|time-zero field> <math|\<varphi\><around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>>
      exists and is Gaussian with covariance
      <math|<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>>: the ground state measure
      <math|\<Omega\><rsub|0><rsup|2>*\<cal-D\>*\<varphi\>> of the free
      Hamiltonian in the Schrödinger representation.

      <item>The GFF is the continuum limit of the lattice spin system of
      Lecture 1 with <math|V=0> (Exercise 3.14).

      <item>Markov property (Nelson): rooted in the <strong|locality of
      <math|A>>.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Nelson's Markov property>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Markov> for <math|\<Lambda\>>: inside and outside are
      conditionally independent given the field near
      <math|\<partial\>*\<Lambda\>>.

      <item><strong|The GFF is Markov for every open <math|\<Lambda\>>>
      (Nelson 1973): <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em><text|outside>|]>=\<varphi\>*<around|(|\<Pi\>*f|)>>,
      and <math|\<Pi\>*f=A*u> is supported on <math|\<partial\>*\<Lambda\>>
      <em|because <math|A=m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>> is local>. Given the outside:
      harmonic extension <math|+> independent Dirichlet GFF (next slide).

      <item><strong|Markov <math|+> sharp <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|0>>
      <math|\<Rightarrow\>> RP:> with <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|0>> the time-zero
      field, <math|\<mu\>> <math|\<Theta\>>-invariant, <math|\<Theta\>>
      trivial on <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|0>>:

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-E\>*<around|[|<wide|\<Theta\>*F|\<bar\>>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\><around*|[|<around|\||\<bbb-E\><around|[|F<space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<Sigma\><rsub|0>|]>|\|><rsup|2>|]>\<geq\>0,<space|2em>\<bbb-E\>[U<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em><around|\||<space|0.17em>\<Sigma\><rsub|0>|]>=\<mathe\><rsup|-t*H><text|on
        >L<rsup|2><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|0>|)>.
      </equation*>

      <vspace*|-4pt>Nelson's reconstruction (1973), before
      Osterwalder\USchrader.

      <item><strong|Relevance:> preserved by local perturbations
      <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int><rsub|\<Lambda\>>V>>
      <math|\<Rightarrow\>> <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> as
      classical statistical mechanics (Guerra\URosen\USimon). Not stable
      under limits; OS use RP instead: not comparable (generalised free
      fields are RP, not Markov; the <math|P<rsup|-1>> field is Markov, not
      RP), but closed under weak limits.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The Markov property in a picture>

    \;

    <image|<tuple|<#>|markov_gff.png>|0.86par|||><next-line><vspace*|2pt>
    Lattice GFF, <math|m<rsup|2>\<approx\>10<rsup|-3>>, <math|\<Lambda\>> a
    disc. Inside <math|\<Lambda\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<varphi\>=h+\<psi\>,<space|2em><around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*h=0*<text|in
      >\<Lambda\>,<space|1em>h=\<varphi\>*<text|on >\<partial\>*\<Lambda\>,
    </equation*>

    <math|h=\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<varphi\><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em><text|outside>|]>>
    depends only on the field <strong|at the boundary>; <math|\<psi\>> is a
    Dirichlet GFF, <strong|independent of the outside>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Symanzik: fields and Brownian paths>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item>Symanzik (1966, 1969): QFT through Schwinger functions as moments
      of a classical Markov field \V the Euclidean programme.

      <item><strong|Paths:> <math|C*<around|(|x,y|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-m<rsup|2>*t>p<rsub|t><around|(|x,y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t>
      is the Green function of a killed Brownian motion. For
      <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int>V>\<mu\><rsub|m>>: a gas of paths and
      loops interacting through local times; for <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4>>,
      intersections are penalised (lattice: Brydges\UFröhlich\USpencer 1982;
      triviality for <math|d\<gtr\>4>).

      <item><strong|Loop soups> (Lawler\UWerner 2004): Poisson cloud of
      Brownian loops of intensity <math|\<alpha\>>; on a graph
      <math|\<bbb-E\><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\<langle\>|\<chi\>,L|\<rangle\>>>=det
      <around|(|1+C*\<chi\>|)><rsup|-\<alpha\>>>.

      <item><strong|Le Jan's isomorphism:> for <math|\<alpha\>=<frac|1|2>>
      the occupation field is <math|<frac|1|2>*\<varphi\><rsup|2>>; in
      <math|d=2,3> the renormalised one is
      <math|<frac|1|2>:\<varphi\><rsup|2>>:.

      <item>Lupu 2016: loop clusters <math|=> sign clusters of the
      cable-graph GFF. Werner 2016: spatial Markov property of loop soups.
      Qian\UWerner 2019. Survey: Werner\UPowell.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Euclidean Källén\ULehmann>

    \;

    Wick rotation of <math|W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|M|)>>>:
    <math|<big|int>\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|M><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|M><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>\|\<tau\>\|+\<mathi\><math-bf|p>\<cdot\><math-bf|x>>=G<rsub|M>*<around|(|\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>>.
    Hence for any theory satisfying the axioms

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|2><around|(|x|)>=v<rsup|2>+<big|int><rsub|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|M><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em><wide|S|^><rsub|2><rsup|T><around|(|k|)>=<big|int><frac|\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+M<rsup|2>>.
    </equation*>

    The GFF is <math|\<rho\>=\<delta\><rsub|m<rsup|2>>>.

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Short distances.> <math|G<rsub|M>> decreases in <math|M>
      and <math|G<rsub|M>/G<rsub|m>\<to\>1> at <math|0> (<math|d\<geq\>2>),
      so <math|liminf<rsub|x\<to\>0> S<rsub|2><rsup|T><around|(|x|)>/G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>\<geq\>\<rho\><around|(|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>|)>\<gtr\>0>:
      never less singular than free. Scale invariance <math|\<Rightarrow\>>
      unitarity bound <math|\<Delta\>\<geq\><frac|d-2|2>> (Exercise 3.2).

      <item><strong|Long distances.> <math|<math-up|supp>\<rho\>\<subset\><around|[|m<rsub|0><rsup|2>,\<infty\>|)>>
      <math|\<Leftrightarrow\>> decay at least like
      <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-m<rsub|0><around|\||x|\|>>>: mass gap <math|=>
      clustering.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Two masses: a positive but nonlocal theory>

    \;

    <math|\<rho\>=c<rsub|1><rsup|2>*\<delta\><rsub|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>*\<delta\><rsub|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>>>,
    <math|0\<less\>m<rsub|1>\<less\>m<rsub|2>>:<space|1em><math|C<rsub|\<rho\>>=c<rsub|1><rsup|2>*<around|(|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>*<around|(|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>>,
    the law of <math|c<rsub|1>*\<varphi\><rsub|1>+c<rsub|2>*\<varphi\><rsub|2>>
    (independent GFFs). RP for each term <math|\<Rightarrow\>> OS axioms.

    <\myframed>
      <strong|It reconstructs to two free species>

      <math|\<cal-H\>=\<Gamma\><around|(|\<frak-h\><rsub|m<rsub|1>>|)>\<otimes\>\<Gamma\><around|(|\<frak-h\><rsub|m<rsub|2>>|)>>,<space|1em><math|\<varphi\>=c<rsub|1>*\<varphi\><rsub|m<rsub|1>>\<otimes\><with|math-font|Bbb|1>+c<rsub|2>*<space|0.17em><with|math-font|Bbb|1>\<otimes\>\<varphi\><rsub|m<rsub|2>>>,<space|1em><math|W<rsub|2>=c<rsub|1><rsup|2>*W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|m<rsub|1>|)>>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>*W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|m<rsub|2>|)>>>.<next-line>Two
      kinds of free particles, created by the same field; no scattering
      (truncated <math|W<rsub|n\<geq\>3>=0>).
    </myframed>

    <with|font-series|bold|What is lost:>

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsub|1><rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsub|2><rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>=0>,
      but no Klein\UGordon equation; <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|0|)>,\<pi\><around|(|0|)>|)>>
      are not complete Cauchy data.

      <item>DS: <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|F*\<varphi\>*<around|(|C<rsub|\<rho\>><rsup|-1>*h|)>|]>>
      with <math|C<rsub|\<rho\>><rsup|-1>=<dfrac|<around|(|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*<around|(|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>|c<rsub|1><rsup|2>*<around|(|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>*<around|(|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>>>
      <strong|nonlocal>; <math|\<varphi\>> is not Markov.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Positivity versus locality>

    \;

    The <strong|local> fourth-order action
    <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,<around|(|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*<around|(|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>
    gives a Markov field with

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsub|1><rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsub|2><rsup|2>|)>>=<frac|1|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-m<rsub|1><rsup|2>>*<around*|(|<frac|1|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsub|1><rsup|2>>-<frac|1|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsub|2><rsup|2>>|)>,
    </equation*>

    so <math|\<rho\>=<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>>-\<delta\><rsub|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>>|)>/<around|(|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-m<rsub|1><rsup|2>|)>>
    has a <strong|negative weight>.

    <\itemize>
      <item>By Euclidean Källén\ULehmann it is <strong|not reflection
      positive>: the mass <math|m<rsub|2>> is a ghost of negative norm
      (Pais\UUhlenbeck, Lee\UWick).

      <item>Same for any <math|1/<wide|C|^>=> polynomial of degree
      <math|\<geq\>2>: <math|s*<space|0.17em><wide|C|^><around|(|s|)>\<to\>0>,
      while <math|s*<big|int><frac|\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>|s+M<rsup|2>>*\<uparrow\>*\<rho\><around|(|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>|)>>.
    </itemize>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Moral>

      For Gaussian fields, <em|positivity> (OS) and <em|locality> of the
      Euclidean field equation (<math|C<rsup|-1>> a differential operator)
      together single out the free field: covariance
      <math|c*<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>> (or white noise).
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Is the nonlocal theory physical?>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|In Minkowski space, yes:> all Wightman axioms hold,
      including locality (W3); positive energy, unique vacuum. Two free
      species: trivial and redundant, not inconsistent.

      <item><strong|``Nonlocal'' is Euclidean:> no Markov property
      <math|\<Leftrightarrow\>> <math|\<varphi\>> alone is not a complete set
      of local degrees of freedom (no Cauchy problem, no Lagrangian in
      <math|\<varphi\>> only). Cure: the pair
      <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|1>,\<varphi\><rsub|2>|)>> is local and
      Markov.

      <item><strong|Continuous <math|\<rho\>>> (a continuum of free species)
      is unphysical: no dynamical law of finite order, and too many localised
      states. Buchholz\UWichmann nuclearity (cf. Haag\USwieca) needs
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>n<rsub|i>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<beta\>*m<rsub|i>>\<less\>\<infty\>>:
      no sensible thermodynamics.

      <item><strong|Interacting theories> also have a continuum in
      <math|\<rho\>>, but produced by a <em|local> interaction: the Euclidean
      field equation of <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>, with
      <math|<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*\<varphi\>+4*\<lambda\>:\<varphi\><rsup|3>>:
      in the DS equation, is local.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <with|font-size|1.6|font-series|bold|color|dark red|Local operators: Wick
    products>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture 2>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Hermite polynomials>

    \;

    Interactions <math|<big|int>V<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>|)>\<mathd\>x>
    need local functions of <math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>>: regularise,
    subtract, remove the regularisation.

    <\equation*>
      \<mathe\><rsup|t*a-c*t<rsup|2>/2>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geq\>0><frac|t<rsup|n>|n!>*H<rsub|n><around|(|a;c|)>:<space|2em>H<rsub|2>=a<rsup|2>-c,H<rsub|3>=a<rsup|3>-3*c*a,H<rsub|4>=a<rsup|4>-6*c*a<rsup|2>+3*c<rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    <\lemma*>
      <math|<around|(|X,Y|)>> centred Gaussian, <math|\<bbb-E\>*X<rsup|2>=a>,
      <math|\<bbb-E\>*Y<rsup|2>=b>:

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|H<rsub|n><around|(|X;a|)>*H<rsub|k><around|(|Y;b|)>|]>=\<delta\><rsub|n*k>*<space|0.17em>n!<space|0.17em><around|(|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|X*Y|]>|)><rsup|n>.
      </equation*>
    </lemma*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Proof.> <math|\<bbb-E\><around*|[|\<mathe\><rsup|s*X-s<rsup|2>*a/2>\<mathe\><rsup|t*Y-t<rsup|2>*b/2>|]>=\<mathe\><rsup|s*t*<space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|X*Y|]>>>;
    compare coefficients of <math|s<rsup|n>*t<rsup|k>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Wick powers>

    \;

    Mollifier <math|\<eta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>,
    <math|\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<eta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<ast\>\<varphi\>>,
    <math|G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>>=\<eta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<ast\>\<eta\><rsub|\<delta\>>\<ast\>G>,
    <math|c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<varepsilon\>><around|(|0|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      :\<varphi\><rsup|n><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>:<around|(|x|)>\<assign\>H<rsub|n><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>,<space|2em>:\<varphi\><rsup|n><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>:<around|(|f|)>=<big|int>f<space|0.17em>:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:.
    </equation*>

    <\theorem*>
      If <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<less\>d> (all <math|n> in <math|d=2>;
      <math|n\<leq\>2> in <math|d=3>), then
      <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>=lim<rsub|\<varepsilon\>\<to\>0>:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>>
      exists in <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|m>|)>>, is independent
      of <math|\<eta\>>, and

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>:\<varphi\><rsup|k>:<around|(|g|)>|]>=\<delta\><rsub|n*k>*<space|0.17em>n!<big|iint>f<around|(|x|)>*G*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|n>*g<around|(|y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>y.
      </equation*>

      If <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<geq\>d> and <math|0\<leq\>f\<nequiv\>0>:
      <math|\<bbb-E\>[:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)><rsup|2>]\<to\>\<infty\>>.
    </theorem*>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Proof>

    \;

    <with|font-series|bold|1. Uniform bound.>
    <math|K<around|(|z|)>=1+log<rsub|+> <frac|1|<around|\||z|\|>>>
    (<math|d=2>), <math|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2-d>> (<math|d\<geq\>3>).
    With <math|r=max <around|(|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>|)>>:

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|<around|\||z|\|>\<geq\>4*r>:
      \ <math|G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>><around|(|z|)>\<leq\>sup<rsub|<around|\||w|\|>\<geq\><around|\||z|\|>/2>
      G<around|(|w|)>\<leq\>C*K<around|(|z|)>>;

      <item><math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>4*r>:
      \ <math|G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>><around|(|z|)>\<leq\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<eta\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*r<rsup|-d>*<big|int><rsub|B<around|(|0,6*r|)>>G\<leq\>C*<frac|1|<around|\||B<around|(|0,6*r|)>|\|>>*<big|int><rsub|B<around|(|0,6*r|)>>K\<leq\>C*K<around|(|r|)>\<leq\>C<rprime|'>*K<around|(|z|)>>.
    </itemize>

    <with|font-series|bold|2. Convergence.> By the lemma,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<nobracket\>|\<bbb-E\><around*|[||(>:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>-:\<varphi\><rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>)<rsup|2>|]>=n!<big|iint>f<around|(|x|)>*f<around|(|y|)>*<around*|(|G<rsup|n><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<varepsilon\>>-2*G<rsup|n><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>>+G<rsup|n><rsub|\<delta\>,\<delta\>>|)>*<around|(|x-y|)>\<to\>0
    </equation*>

    by dominated convergence: <math|K<rsup|n>\<in\>L<rsup|1><rsub|<math-up|loc>>\<Longleftrightarrow\>n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<less\>d>.

    <with|font-series|bold|3. Divergence.> Otherwise
    <math|G<rsup|n>\<gtrsim\><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-n*<around|(|d-2|)>>\<nin\>L<rsup|1><rsub|<math-up|loc>>>;
    Fatou.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Remarks>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Renormalisation:> <math|\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x|)><rsup|n>>
      has no limit; subtracting <math|c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<to\>\<infty\>>
      (via <math|H<rsub|n>>) is the simplest counterterm. A different
      mollifier changes <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>>: by lower Wick powers with
      <em|finite> coefficients (Exercise 3.4).

      <item><strong|Chaos:> <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>> lies in
      the <math|n>-th Wiener chaos; <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|m>|)>\<simeq\>>
      Fock space over <math|H<rsup|-1><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>.
      Hypercontractivity <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<leq\><around|(|p-1|)><rsup|n/2><around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|2>>>
      gives convergence in all <math|L<rsup|p>> (and in
      <math|\<cal-C\><rsup|-\<kappa\>>> for <math|d=2>).

      <item><strong|Towards <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>:> on
      <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>>, <math|<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>:\<in\><big|cap><rsub|p>L<rsup|p>>
      and (Nelson) <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<lambda\>*<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>:>\<in\>L<rsup|1><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|m>|)>>.

      <item><strong|Other local operators:>
      <math|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*\<varphi\>>; in <math|d=2> the Wick
      exponentials <math|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<beta\>*\<varphi\>>>:, in
      <math|L<rsup|2>> iff <math|\<beta\><rsup|2>\<less\>4*\<pi\>>
      (sine-Gordon; Exercise 3.6).
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <with|font-size|1.6|font-series|bold|color|dark red|The Dyson\USchwinger
    equation>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture 2>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Beyond <math|L<rsup|2>>: Wick powers do not exist>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item>If <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<geq\>d>:
      <math|\<bbb-E\>[:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)><rsup|2>]\<to\>\<infty\>>.
      Worse: there is <strong|no random distribution> at all, in any topology
      or on any space. Albeverio\ULiang 2007:
      <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|2>:<around|(|f|)>>, <math|f\<gtr\>0>, in
      <math|d=4> (and <math|d\<geq\>5>) is a Hida distribution, not a random
      variable.

      <item><strong|Proof.> <math|X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|n>>,
      <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<to\>\<infty\>>,
      <math|\<bbb-E\>*X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|4>\<leq\>9<rsup|n>*\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|4>>.
      Paley\UZygmund, <math|\<bbb-P\>*<around|(|Z\<gtr\>\<theta\>*\<bbb-E\>*Z|)>\<geq\><around|(|1-\<theta\>|)><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-E\>*Z|)><rsup|2>/\<bbb-E\>*Z<rsup|2>>,
      with <math|Z=X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>>:

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-P\>*<around*|(|<around|\||X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|\|>\<gtr\><tfrac|1|2>\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<geq\><tfrac|9|16><space|0.17em>9<rsup|-n><space|1em>\<Rightarrow\><space|1em><text|not
        tight.>
      </equation*>

      <item><strong|Bounded chaos: the law is controlled by
      <math|\<sigma\>=<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>>.> Upper tails
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leq\><around|(|p-1|)><rsup|n/2>*\<sigma\>>
      (hypercontractivity); <math|\<bbb-P\>*<around|(|<around|\||X|\|>\<gtr\><frac|1|2>*\<sigma\>|)>\<geq\>c<rsub|n>>
      (Paley\UZygmund); <math|\<bbb-P\>*<around|(|<around|\||X|\|>\<leq\>t*\<sigma\>|)>\<leq\>C*n*t<rsup|1/n>>
      (Carbery\UWright). So: tight <math|\<Leftrightarrow\>> bounded in
      <math|L<rsup|2>>; in probability <math|\<Leftrightarrow\>> in
      <math|L<rsup|2>>.

      <item>Renormalising by <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|-1>>
      typically gives Gaussian limits (fourth moment theorem; Albeverio\UZhou
      1996): the <math|n>-th power is lost.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Gaussian integration by parts>

    \;

    Cylinder functions <math|\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>\<ni\>F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=g*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)>>,
    <math|g\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>>:

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|h>*F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<around*|.|<frac|\<mathd\>|\<mathd\>t>*F*<around|(|\<varphi\>+t*h|)>|\|><rsub|t=0>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g<around|(|\<ldots\>|)><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>,h|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|D*F,h|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>D*F<around|(|x|)>=<frac|\<delta\>*F|\<delta\>*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>>.
    </equation*>

    <\lemma*>
      <math|<around|(|Y,X<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,X<rsub|n>|)>> centred
      Gaussian:<space|1em><math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|Y*<space|0.17em>g<around|(|X|)>|]>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|Y*X<rsub|i>|]>*<space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<partial\><rsub|i>*g<around|(|X|)>|]>>.
    </lemma*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Proof.> For <math|g=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\><around|\<langle\>|u,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>>:
    <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|Y\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\><around|\<langle\>|u,X|\<rangle\>>>|]>=-\<mathi\>\<partial\><rsub|\<theta\>>*\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<theta\>*Y+\<mathi\><around|\<langle\>|u,X|\<rangle\>>>|]>\|<rsub|\<theta\>=0>=\<mathi\><big|sum><rsub|i>u<rsub|i>*\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|Y*X<rsub|i>|]>*\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\><around|\<langle\>|u,X|\<rangle\>>>|]>>;
    then Fourier inversion and approximation.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The Dyson\USchwinger equation>

    \;

    <\proposition*>
      For all <math|F\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>>,
      <math|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>:

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>>*<around*|[|F*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*h|)>|]>,<space|2em><text|formally><space|2em>\<bbb-E\><around*|[|<frac|\<delta\>*F|\<delta\>*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>>|]>=\<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|F*<space|0.17em><around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>|]>.
      </equation*>
    </proposition*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Proof.> <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*h|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|i>|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|A*h,C*f<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|h,f<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>>;
    apply the lemma.

    With <math|F=\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|2>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>>
    one gets the hierarchy

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|x<rsub|1>>|)>*S<rsub|n><around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=2><rsup|n>\<delta\>*<around|(|x<rsub|1>-x<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|n-2><around|(|\<ldots\><wide|x<rsub|j>|^>\<ldots\>|)>,
    </equation*>

    which with <math|S<rsub|0>=1>, <math|S<rsub|1>=0> gives Wick's theorem
    (Exercise 3.7).
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|DS <math|=> Euclidean field equation>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item>Minkowski: <math|<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>=0> and
      CCR.<space|1em>Euclidean: <math|<around|(|-\<Delta\>+m<rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>>
      \P<math|=>\Q <math|\<delta\>/\<delta\>*\<varphi\>> inside expectations;
      the contact terms come from the CCR (time ordering).

      <item>For a formal Gibbs measure <math|\<mu\><rsub|V>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int>V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsub|m>>:

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|V>>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|V>>*<around*|[|F*<space|0.17em><around*|(|<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*\<varphi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|)><around|(|h|)>|]>,
      </equation*>

      the Euclidean version of <math|<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=0>.

      <item><math|V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>> must be a well-defined
      local operator: <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>:
      <math|V<rprime|'>=4*\<lambda\>:\<varphi\><rsup|3>>:; field equation for
      <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>>: Dimock\UGlimm 1974,
      Albeverio\URöckner 1991. For <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> the
      renormalised field equation goes back to Feldman\UR¡czka 1977;
      renormalised cubic term as a random distribution: Gubinelli\UHofmanová
      2021.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Defining <math|V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>: point
    splitting and the OPE>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|\<varphi\><rsup|3><around|(|x|)>>: product at one point.
      <strong|Point splitting>: regular part of
      <math|\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|1>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|2>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|3>|)>>
      as <math|y<rsub|i>\<to\>x>.

      <item>Under the GFF (Wick):

      <\equation*>
        \<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|1>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|2>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|3>|)>=:\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|1>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|2>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|3>|)>:+<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>j>G*<around|(|y<rsub|i>-y<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|k>|)>
      </equation*>

      <vspace*|-3pt>regular part <math|\<to\>:\<varphi\><rsup|3>:<around|(|x|)>>;
      mollified: <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|3>\<assign\>lim<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|3>-3*c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>>.

      <item><strong|OPE> (Wilson 1969, Zimmermann 1973): products at nearby
      points <math|=> local operators <math|\<times\>> singular coefficients;
      composite operator <math|=> regular part. Field equation
      <math|<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>=-4*\<lambda\>*N<around|[|\<varphi\><rsup|3>|]>>.

      <item>The coefficients are those of the <strong|interacting> theory.
      <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>: Gaussian subtraction suffices (Schrader
      1974). <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>: an extra
      <math|\<delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>,
      <math|\<delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<sim\>\<lambda\>*log
      <around|(|1/\<varepsilon\>|)>>: <math|4*\<lambda\>*\<delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>
      is the sunset mass counterterm (Gubinelli\UHofmanová 2021).

      <item>Lecture 3: OPE of stochastic observables, explicit at every
      scale.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|DS characterises the GFF>

    \;

    <\theorem*>
      Let <math|\<nu\>> be a probability measure on
      <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> with
      <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>\<in\>L<rsup|1><around|(|\<nu\>|)>> for
      all <math|f> and

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|[|F*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*h|)>|]><space|2em>\<forall\>F\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>,h\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>.
      </equation*>

      Then <math|\<nu\>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>.
    </theorem*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Proof.> Fix <math|f>, take
    <math|h=C*f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> and
    <math|F<rsub|s>=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>s*\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>>, so
    <math|D<rsub|h>*F<rsub|s>=\<mathi\>s*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>*F<rsub|s>>.
    With <math|\<chi\><around|(|s|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>*F<rsub|s>>:

    <\equation*>
      \<mathi\>s*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>*\<chi\><around|(|s|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>><around*|[|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>s*\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>|]>=-\<mathi\>\<chi\><rprime|'><around|(|s|)><space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em>\<chi\><around|(|s|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|s<rsup|2>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>.
    </equation*>

    So <math|<wide|\<nu\>|^><around|(|f|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>
    for all <math|f>; linearity of <math|f\<mapsto\>\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>
    and Cramér\UWold give all finite-dimensional laws.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Remarks>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item>The integrability assumption is exactly what is needed for DS to
      make sense: <math|\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*h|)>> ranges over all
      <math|\<varphi\><around|(|g|)>>.

      <item>DS for characteristic functions is a <strong|first-order linear
      ODE>.

      <item><math|m=0> on the torus: <em|no> probability measure solves DS
      (take <math|h=1>); work modulo constants (Exercise 3.9).

      <item>Finite dimensions: DS says <math|\<nabla\><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|S>\<nu\>|)>=0>,
      so uniqueness is easy for any <math|S> (Exercise 3.8).

      <item>Interacting theories: DS is a singular nonlinear relation;
      uniqueness is hard and largely open. Results for the exponential model
      in <math|d=2>: De Vecchi\UGubinelli\UTurra.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <with|font-size|1.6|font-series|bold|color|dark red|Parabolic stochastic
    quantisation>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture 2>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Langevin dynamics (Parisi\UWu 1981)>

    \;

    Finite dimensions: <math|\<mathd\>X<rsub|t>=-\<nabla\>*S<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>\<mathd\>t+<sqrt|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>B<rsub|t>>
    has generator <math|\<cal-L\>=\<Delta\>-\<nabla\>*S\<cdot\>\<nabla\>> and

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><rsup|\<ast\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-S>=\<nabla\>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<nabla\>\<mathe\><rsup|-S>+\<mathe\><rsup|-S>\<nabla\>*S|)>=0:
    </equation*>

    the Gibbs measure <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-S>\<mathd\>x/Z> is invariant.

    Apply this to the Euclidean action, with a fictitious <strong|stochastic
    time> <math|t>:

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Phi\>=-<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*\<Phi\>-V<rprime|'><around|(|\<Phi\>|)>+<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\>*<space|2em><text|on
      >\<bbb-R\><rsub|+>\<times\>\<cal-M\>,
    </equation*>

    <math|\<xi\>> space-time white noise:
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<xi\><around|(|u|)>*\<xi\><around|(|v|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>><rsub|L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>>.

    For <math|V=0>: the stochastic heat equation (infinite-dimensional OU
    process).

    History: Parisi\UWu 1981 (gauge theories without gauge fixing); physics
    review Damgaard\UHüffel 1987\U88; first mathematical construction for
    <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>>:
    Jona-Lasinio\UMitter 1985. Review: Gubinelli, <em|Encyclopedia of
    Mathematical Physics> 2025.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|DS <math|\<Rightarrow\>> invariance>

    \;

    Itô's formula: for <math|F=g*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)>>
    the generator is

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\>*F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>,f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i*j>*g<around|(|\<ldots\>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*f<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g<around|(|\<ldots\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    <\proposition*>
      <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>>*<around|[|G*<space|0.17em>\<cal-L\>*F|]>=-\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>><around*|[|<around|\<langle\>|D*G,D*F|\<rangle\>><rsub|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>|]>>
      for <math|F,G\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>>; in particular
      <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>>*<around|[|\<cal-L\>*F|]>=0>.
    </proposition*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Proof.> Apply DS to
    <math|k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g> with <math|h=f<rsub|i>> and
    sum over <math|i>:

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i>\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*f<rsub|i>|)>|]>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>,f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>*\<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|\<partial\><rsub|j>*k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g+k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i*j>*g|]>.<space|2em>
    </equation*>

    <strong|Static (DS) and dynamic (Langevin) characterisations are two
    faces of the same integration by parts.>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The stationary solution>

    \;

    <\theorem*>
      For <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>
      let <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>=<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\><around|(|K<rsub|t,f>|)>>,
      \ <math|K<rsub|t,f><around|(|r,x|)>=<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|r\<less\>t><around*|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-r|)>*A>f|)><around|(|x|)>>.

      <\enumerate-alpha>
        <item><math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>*\<Phi\><rsub|s><around|(|g|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\||t-s|\|>*A>C*g|\<rangle\>>>:
        stationary, and <math|<math-up|Law><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>
        for every <math|t>.

        <item><math|\<Phi\>> is a mild and weak solution: for
        <math|s\<less\>t>, <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|h|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|s><around|(|h|)>-<big|int><rsub|s><rsup|t>\<Phi\><rsub|r>*<around|(|A*h|)>\<mathd\>r+<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\>*<around|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|(|s,t|)>>\<otimes\>h|)>>.

        <item>From any initial datum <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>
        independent of <math|\<xi\>>: <math|\<bbb-E\><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<Psi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>>\<to\>\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>.
        Hence <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> is the <strong|unique invariant measure>.
      </enumerate-alpha>
    </theorem*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Proof of (a).>
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|K<rsub|t,f>,K<rsub|s,g>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|-\<infty\>><rsup|t\<wedge\>s><around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t+s-2*r|)>*A>g|\<rangle\>>\<mathd\>r=<around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\||t-s|\|>*A><around|(|2*A|)><rsup|-1>*g|\<rangle\>>>;
    an <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'>>-valued version exists by Itô\UNawata/Mitoma
    regularisation.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Proof of (b), (c)>

    \;

    <with|font-series|bold|(a), on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d>>.> The modes
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|e<rsub|n>|)>> are independent stationary
    OU processes <math|\<mathd\>X=-\<lambda\><rsub|n>*X\<mathd\>t+<sqrt|2>\<mathd\>B>
    with variance <math|\<lambda\><rsub|n><rsup|-1>>.

    <with|font-series|bold|(b)> Mild form from the semigroup identity
    <math|K<rsub|t,h>=K<rsub|s,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-s|)>*A>h>+<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|(|s,t|)>>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-\<cdummy\>|)>*A>h>;
    weak form by stochastic Fubini (Exercise 3.12).

    <with|font-series|bold|(c)> By independence,

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<Psi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>>=\<bbb-E\><around*|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<Psi\><rsub|0><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f|)>>|]><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,<around|(|1-\<mathe\><rsup|-2*t*A>|)>*C*f|\<rangle\>>>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|m\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f\<to\>0> in
    <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>, so
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f|)>\<to\>0> a.s. If
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsub|m>> the product is exactly
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>
    (invariance); an invariant <math|\<nu\>> must have the same
    characteristic functional.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Comments>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item>As a field on <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>, the stationary
      solution has covariance <math|2*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|t>+A|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|t>+A|)><rsup|-1>=2*<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|2>+A<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>;
      its equal-time slice is <math|2*<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>><around|(|\<theta\><rsup|2>+A<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>*<frac|\<mathd\>\<theta\>|2*\<pi\>>=A<rsup|-1>>.

      <item>The stochastic time <math|t> has nothing to do with the Euclidean
      time <math|\<tau\>>.

      <item>Interacting case: a <strong|singular SPDE>.

      <\itemize>
        <item><math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>: Da Prato\UDebussche 2003 (also
        Jona-Lasinio\UMitter, Albeverio\URöckner).

        <item><math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>: Hairer 2014;
        Gubinelli\UImkeller\UPerkowski 2015; Catellier\UChouk 2018.

        <item>Global bounds: Mourrat\UWeber 2017; Gubinelli\UHofmanová 2019;
        construction of Euclidean <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> via the
        dynamics: Gubinelli\UHofmanová 2021.
      </itemize>
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <with|font-size|1.6|font-series|bold|color|dark red|Elliptic stochastic
    quantisation>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture 2>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Dimensional reduction (Parisi\USourlas 1979)>

    \;

    The elliptic SPDE in <math|d+2> dimensions

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>|)>*\<Psi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<Psi\>|)>=\<zeta\>,<space|2em>\<zeta\>*<text|white
      noise on >\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>,
    </equation*>

    is related to the <math|d>-dimensional Gibbs measure: formally

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|Law><around*|(|\<Psi\>(z,\<cdot\><around*|)||)>\<propto\>exp
      <around*|(|-4*\<pi\>*<around*|[|<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,A*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>+<big|int>V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|]>|)>*\<cal-D\>*\<varphi\>.|\<nobracket\>>
    </equation*>

    <\itemize>
      <item>Mechanism: a hidden <strong|supersymmetry> (Parisi\USourlas;
      Klein\ULandau\UPerez 1984).

      <item>Rigorous nonlinear results: <math|d=0> (Albeverio\UDe
      Vecchi\UGubinelli 2020); polynomial on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>> and
      exponential on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>> (ADG 2021); elliptic
      <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>> in 4 and 5 dimensions (Gubinelli\UHofmanová
      2019).

      <item>For <math|V=0> it is elementary, and it explains the
      <math|4*\<pi\>>.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The linear case>

    \;

    <\theorem*>
      Let <math|<math-ss|L>=m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>=-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>+A>
      and <math|\<zeta\>> white noise on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>.

      <\enumerate-alpha>
        <item><math|<math-ss|L>\<Psi\>=<sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\>>
        has a unique tempered solution <math|\<Psi\><around|(|u|)>=<sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\><around|(|<math-ss|L><rsup|-1>u|)>>.

        <item><math|K<rsub|z,f>=<math-ss|L><rsup|-1><around|(|\<delta\><rsub|z>\<otimes\>f|)>\<in\>L<rsup|2>>,
        continuous in <math|z>; <math|\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)>=<sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\><around|(|K<rsub|z,f>|)>>
        is the section of <math|\<Psi\>> at <math|z>.

        <item><math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)>*\<Psi\><around|(|z,g|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,C*g|\<rangle\>>>:
        every section has law <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>, and
        <math|(\<Psi\>(z,\<cdot\><around|)||)><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>>
        is stationary and isotropic.
      </enumerate-alpha>
    </theorem*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Uniqueness:> <math|<math-ss|L>> maps
    <math|\<cal-S\>> onto <math|\<cal-S\>>, so
    <math|D<around|(|<math-ss|L>u|)>=0> <math|\<forall\>u> forces <math|D=0>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Proof: where <math|4*\<pi\>> comes from>

    \;

    Fourier transform in <math|z> only: <math|<wide|K<rsub|z,f>|^><around|(|q|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>q\<cdot\>z><around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-1>*f>.
    By Plancherel and the spectral theorem,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|K<rsub|z,f>,K<rsub|z,g>|\<rangle\>>=<around*|\<langle\>|f,<around*|[|<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>*<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-2>|]>*g|\<rangle\>>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\>>*<around|\<langle\>|f,A<rsup|-1>*g|\<rangle\>>,
    </equation*>

    since

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>*<frac|1|<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+a|)><rsup|2>>=<frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|r<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>r|<around|(|r<rsup|2>+a|)><rsup|2>>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\>*a>.
    </equation*>

    Multiply by <math|4*\<pi\>>. Stationarity and isotropy: the covariance
    <math|4*\<pi\>*<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>q\<cdot\><around|(|z-z<rprime|'>|)>><around|\<langle\>|f,<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-2>*g|\<rangle\>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>>
    depends only on <math|<around|\||z-z<rprime|'>|\|>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Slices of Gaussian fields>

    \;

    Field on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> with covariance
    <math|<around|(|-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>>+A|)><rsup|-p>>; its
    slice <math|<around|{|0|}>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> has covariance

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|k,p>*<space|0.17em>A<rsup|k/2-p>,<space|2em>c<rsub|k,p>=<frac|\<Gamma\>*<around|(|p-<frac|k|2>|)>|<around|(|4*\<pi\>|)><rsup|k/2>*\<Gamma\><around|(|p|)>>.
    </equation*>

    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|5|5|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<math|<around|(|k,p|)>>>|<cell|slice
    covariance>|<cell|interpretation>>|<row|<cell|<math|<around|(|1,1|)>>>|<cell|<math|<frac|1|2>*A<rsup|-1/2>>>|<cell|time-zero
    field, <math|<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>>>>|<row|<cell|<math|<around|(|2,2|)>>>|<cell|<math|<around|(|4*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-1>*A<rsup|-1>>>|<cell|elliptic
    stochastic quantisation>>|<row|<cell|parabolic>|<cell|<math|2*<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|2>+A<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>\<to\>A<rsup|-1>>>|<cell|Langevin
    dynamics>>|<row|<cell|<math|<around|(|1,2|)>>>|<cell|<math|\<propto\>A<rsup|-3/2>>>|<cell|not
    a GFF (e.g. codim-1 slices of the elliptic field)>>>>>

    The GFF arises iff <math|p=1+<frac|k|2>>. For
    <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, every codimension-two slice of the
    elliptic field is a GFF.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Outlook>

    \;

    <with|font-series|bold|Next lecture>

    <\itemize>
      <item>Lecture 3: the GFF along a scale parameter; stochastic
      observables; Polchinski equation and its FBSDE; Wetterich equation;
      variational structure.

      <item>Later lectures (not yet settled): probably a construction of
      <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on the torus via the FBSDE.
    </itemize>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|The pattern>

      Gaussian measure <math|<long-arrow|\<rubber-rightarrow\>|<text|local
      perturbation>>> renormalised DS equation / scale dynamics
      <math|<long-arrow|\<rubber-rightarrow\>|<text|estimates>>> Euclidean
      QFT <math|<long-arrow|\<rubber-rightarrow\>|<text|OS>>> relativistic
      QFT.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Exercises and reading>

    \;

    <wide-tabular|<tformat|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-lsep|0em>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-rsep|1em>|<cwith|1|1|1|1|cell-width|0.5par>|<cwith|1|1|1|1|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|1|2|2|cell-width|0.46par>|<cwith|1|1|2|2|cell-hmode|exact>|<table|<row|<\cell>
      <with|font-series|bold|Exercises> (numbering of the notes)

      <\itemize>
        <item>3.2 Unitarity bound.

        <item>3.3 Regularity is sharp.

        <item>3.6 Wick exponentials, <math|\<beta\><rsup|2>\<less\>4*\<pi\>>.

        <item>3.7 Wick's theorem from DS.

        <item>3.8 DS in finite dimensions.

        <item>3.10 Infinitesimal invariance characterises
        <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>.

        <item>3.13 Slices of Gaussian fields.

        <item>3.15 Computer experiments.

        <item>3.16 Two masses: positivity vs. locality.
